    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Atypical features and mimics of malignancy in pulmonary cytopathological preparations {27221749; 30156772; 12820712; 28659503; 20607685}</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Ash Chandra and Paul VanderLaan",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"width: 695.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" width=\"927\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 134.85pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 134.85pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Nonspecific cellular changes due to inflammatory conditions:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Loss of architectural polarity in epithelial tissue fragments associated with nuclear crowding and disorganization</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Cytomegaly and anisonucleosis within tissue fragments</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Reactive bronchial cells showing nuclear enlargement and oval nuclei with few minor nuclear membrane irregularities or indentations, although usually showing evenly distributed chromatin and nucleoli; these may lack cilia (ciliocytophthoria); the nuclei tend to show uniform changes, including nucleolar enlargement, across any particular tissue fragment</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Inflammatory debris mimicking tumour type necrosis; there is usually a prominent inflammatory cell component, varying from predominantly neutrophils to histiocytes or granulomatous epithelioid histiocytes</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 121.05pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 121.05pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Reserve cell hyperplasia due to chronic irritation and inflammation:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Usually encountered in bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and bronchial brushing specimens</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Occurs as small cohesive sheets and more 3D tissue fragments of small cells with an increased N:C&nbsp;ratio; hyperchromatic, small, round to mildly pleomorphic nuclei with uniform coarse chromatin and single nucleoli; scant cytoplasm; no cilia are present</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">May mimic neuroendocrine tumours (NETs)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Usually cellularity is low, with only a small number of atypical epithelial tissue fragments present in a background of prominent histiocytes</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 124.9pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 124.9pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Squamous metaplasia:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Occurs due to chronic irritation and/or infections, including abscesses and fungal or viral infections</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Round to oval squamous cells with hyperchromatic, round to elongated nuclei and a variable but often low N:C&nbsp;ratio</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Can be encountered in all types of respiratory cytopathology specimens</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Can mimic well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma: difficult differential diagnosis in the setting of possible residual carcinoma after radiotherapy, when need to assess changes related to therapy</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Associated keratinous debris can mimic tumour necrosis</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 107.25pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 107.25pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Postradiotherapy and postchemotherapy changes:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Usually low cellularity with scattered epithelial tissue fragments showing a degree of irregularity in the cell arrangement</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Readily identifiable cytomegaly with low N:C ratio and vacuolization, nucleomegaly, and nuclear pleomorphism involving all cellular elements of the respiratory tract</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Hyperchromatic nuclei with varying degrees of blurring of chromatin and vacuoles</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Foamy histiocytes are seen in amiodarone-associated lung injury</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 93.45pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 93.45pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Hyperplasia and reactive changes of type&nbsp;2 pneumocytes:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Encountered in acute respiratory distress syndrome, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and viral infections</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Enlarged nuclei with vesicular chromatin and prominent nucleoli</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Crowded tissue fragments (Creola bodies) with a degree of nuclear overlapping, mimicking malignancy</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Abundant clear cytoplasm, mimicking mucinous adenocarcinoma</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 121.05pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 695.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 121.05pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"927\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 22.9pt; text-indent: -22.9pt;\"><strong>6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><span style=\"color: black;\">Viral infections:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Encountered with herpes simplex virus, CMV, and other viral infections</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Cytomegaly and enlarged round to oval to mildly pleomorphic nuclei</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Abnormal nuclear chromatin showing margination and clearing of viral inclusions, and (particularly in herpetic infections) multinucleated epithelial cells; CMV shows classic owl-eye nuclei</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Bronchial cells with reparative changes including nuclear enlargement, prominent nucleoli, and ciliocytophthoria; nuclei tend to be relatively uniform in size and shape</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  }
]